{{2022 Australian federal election sidebar}}
The '''2022 Australian federal election''' was held on 21 May 2022 to elect members of the [[47th Parliament of Australia]]. The incumbent [[Coalition (Australia)|Liberal/National coalition]] [[Morrison government|government]] led by [[Prime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]] [[Scott Morrison]] sought to win a fourth consecutive term in office but was defeated by the [[Opposition (Australia)|opposition]], the [[Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]] led by [[Anthony Albanese]]. The [[Australian Greens|Greens]], [[United Australia Party (2013)|United Australia Party]], [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation|One Nation]], other minor parties, and [[Independent politician|independents]] also contested the election. Up for election were all 151 seats in the lower house, the [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], and 40 of the 76 seats in the upper house, the [[Australian Senate|Senate]].

The election resulted in severe losses for the Coalition, which won its lowest share of seats in the House of Representatives since [[1946 Australian federal election|1946]], the first election contested by the Liberals as the Coalition's senior partner.<ref>{{cite news |last=Curtis |first=Katina |title=Liberals at lowest proportion of seats since party's first poll in 1946 |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/liberals-at-lowest-proportion-of-seats-since-party-s-first-poll-in-1946-20220524-p5ao00.html |access-date=25 May 2022 |newspaper=The Sydney Morning Herald |date=24 May 2022}}</ref> At 10:00 pm [[Time in Australia|AEST]] ([[UTC+10]]) on election night, [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] projected that only the Labor Party could realistically form a government,<ref name="ABC20220521Worthington">{{cite news |last1=Worthington |first1=Brett |title=Labor takes election victory, incoming PM Anthony Albanese wants Australians to 'reach for the stars' |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-21/labor-anthony-albanese-projected-to-win-2022-federal-election/101084660 |access-date=22 May 2022 |work=ABC News |date=21 May 2022}}</ref> and nine days later projected Labor had secured an outright [[Majority government|majority]] of seats in the House of Representatives.<ref name=76seatscalledABC>{{cite news|last1=Worthington|first1=Brett|title=Anthony Albanese and Labor to form majority government with projected win in Macnamara|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-30/anthony-albanese-and-labor-to-form-majority-government/101084236|access-date=30 May 2022|date=30 May 2022|work=ABC News}}</ref> All states and territories except [[Tasmania]] showed a swing in two-party-preferred vote from the Coalition to Labor, with the largest two-party-preferred swing being in [[Western Australia]] (10%). 

While the Coalition was soundly defeated, the results did not translate into a [[landslide victory]] for Labor due to electoral successes by the Greens and independent candidates. Several formerly safe Coalition seats in urban and suburban areas, including many that had been in non-Labor hands for decades, were won by [[teal independents]]. Several of these independents unseated Liberal incumbents, including Treasurer and Deputy Liberal Leader [[Josh Frydenberg]]. The Greens also won more than one lower house seat for the first time in their history due to increased support nationwide, with three seat gains in inner-city [[Brisbane]]. The combined major party vote for Labor and the Coalition was the lowest on record with 68.5%, while the minor party and independent vote was at its highest at 31.5%.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/datablog/2022/may/28/votes-for-labor-and-the-coalition-plummet-to-all-time-low-as-australia-swings-away-from-major-parties |title=Votes for Labor and the Coalition plummet to all-time low as Australia swings away from major parties |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |location=London |issn=0261-3077 |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=2022-06-01}}</ref> 

Late on election night, Morrison conceded that the Coalition was no longer able to form even a [[minority government]], and announced his forthcoming resignation as Liberal leader.<ref>{{cite news |date=21 May 2022 |title=Live: Morrison calls Albanese to concede electoral defeat as Labor, independents unseat Coalition |publisher=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-21/federal-election-live-blog-scott-morrison-anthony-albanese/101085640 |access-date=21 May 2022}}</ref> Albanese was sworn in as Prime Minister on 23 May 2022.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.skynews.com.au/australia-news/politics/watch-live-anthony-albanese-sworn-in-as-australias-31st-prime-minister-in-canberra/news-story/cd751190142d42580ad141f77b123001|title=Anthony Albanese sworn in as Australia's 31st Prime Minister in Canberra following historic Labor election victory|first=Tyrone|last=Clarke|publisher=Sky News|date=23 May 2022|accessdate=23 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-23/anthony-albanese-sworn-in-as-31st-prime-minister/13894004|title=Video: Anthony Albanese sworn in as 31st Prime Minister of Australia|publisher=Australia Broadcasting Corporation|date=23 May 2022|accessdate=23 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-21/labor-anthony-albanese-projected-to-win-2022-federal-election/101084660|title=With Anthony Albanese at the helm, Labor is projected to win 2022 federal election|last=Worthington|first=Brett|date=22 May 2022|newspaper=The Guardian|access-date=22 May 2022}}</ref> With projections reached in all divisions, Labor won 77 seats, enough for a three seat majority, which would be reduced to two if they elect their own [[Speaker of the Australian House of Representatives|speaker]]. The Coalition fell to 58 seats, the Greens claimed their highest amount of lower house seats with four, and the 12 other seats on the [[Crossbencher|crossbench]] were won by independents and minor party MPs. The result meant Labor achieved their first parliamentary majority since 2007.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fuller |first1=Kelly |last2=Fernandez |first2=Tim |title=Labor wins marginal NSW seat of Gilmore, no more lower house seats in doubt |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-31/labor-projected-win-gilmore-fiona-phillips/101113882 |website=ABC News |date=31 May 2022 |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |access-date=1 June 2022}}</ref>

In the upper house [[Australian Senate|Senate]], Labor is on track to win 15 seats and retain its 26 seat-share in the chamber. The Coalition is currently on 31 seats, a five-seat drop from the previous parliament, while the Greens are expected to win a seat in every state and finish with 12 members, which would be the biggest number of Greens Senators in Australian history. One Nation is on track to retain two seats, the [[Jacqui Lambie Network]] is likely to win an additional seat in Tasmania and thus have two Senators, whilst independent candidate [[David Pocock]] is expected to win the second of two seats in the [[Australian Capital Territory]] (the first time an ACT senator elected was not a Labor or Liberal party member). There are currently two seats listed as in doubt by ABC News in Victoria and South Australia with the Liberals ahead. If the Coalition gets the two seats in doubt, Labor will need 13 votes from a crossbench of 17 (including 12 Greens) to pass legislation the Coalition would not support.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/federal/2022/results/senate|title=Senate Results|work=ABC News|date=21 May 2022|access-date=2 June 2022}}</ref>

Albanese is the fourth Labor leader to lead the party into government from opposition since [[World War II]], after [[Gough Whitlam]] in [[1972 Australian federal election|1972]], [[Bob Hawke]] in [[1983 Australian federal election|1983]], and [[Kevin Rudd]] in [[2007 Australian federal election|2007]].

==Background==
===Previous election===
{{main|2019 Australian federal election}}
{{see also|Results of the 2019 Australian federal election (House of Representatives)}}
{{see also|Results of the 2019 Australian federal election (Senate)}}
At the previous election in May 2019, the [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal]]/[[National Party of Australia|National]] [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]], led by [[Scott Morrison]], formed government winning 77 seats in the House of Representatives, enough for a three-seat majority,<ref name="greenblog">{{cite web |last=Green |first=Antony |title=Exposing a Rubbish Statistic – there were more Labor than Liberal voters at the 2019 Federal election |url=https://antonygreen.com.au/exposing-a-rubbish-statistic-there-were-more-labor-than-liberal-voters-in-2019/ |website=Antony Green's Election Blog |access-date=11 April 2022 |archive-date=18 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220318214954/https://antonygreen.com.au/exposing-a-rubbish-statistic-there-were-more-labor-than-liberal-voters-in-2019/ |url-status=live}}</ref> whilst Labor claimed 68 seats and remained in [[Opposition (Australia)|opposition]].<ref name="greenblog"/> A further six seats were won by other parties and [[Independent politician|independents]], one each to the [[Australian Greens|Greens]], [[Centre Alliance]], and [[Katter's Australian Party]], and the remaining three by independents forming the [[Crossbencher|crossbench]].<ref name="greenblog"/> In the Senate, the Coalition made modest gains in most states and increased their share of seats to 35 overall, whilst Labor remained steady on 26, the Greens likewise on 9, [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation|One Nation]] and Centre Alliance down to 2 each, and [[Jacqui Lambie]] and [[Cory Bernardi]]'s minor parties with 1 seat each.<ref name="apl2019"/> This meant the Coalition required four additional votes to pass legislation.<ref name="apl2019">{{cite web |last=Muller |first=Damon |title=The 2019 federal election |url=https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/rp1920/2019FederalElection |publisher=Australian Parliamentary Library |access-date=10 April 2022 |archive-date=10 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220410221507/https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/pubs/rp/rp1920/2019FederalElection |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Composition of parliament===
{{see also|46th Parliament of Australia#Membership changes}}
The [[46th Parliament of Australia|46th Parliament]] was inaugurated on 2 July 2019. By this time the Labor Party had elected a new leader, replacing the outgoing [[Bill Shorten]] with [[Anthony Albanese]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Yosufzai |first=Rashida |title=Bill Shorten will step down as Labor leader after election defeat |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/news/bill-shorten-will-step-down-as-labor-leader-after-election-defeat |date=1 May 2019 |website=SBS News}} {{verify source |date=September 2019 |reason=This ref was deleted Special:Diff/898247597 by a bug in VisualEditor and later restored by a bot from the original cite located at Special:Permalink/898239040 cite #3 – verify the cite is accurate and delete this template. [[User:GreenC_bot/Job_18]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-05-27/anthony-albanese-the-new-labor-leader-following-election-loss/11152036?section=politics|title=Anthony Albanese to become Labor's new leader unopposed following shock federal election loss|date=27 May 2019|publisher=ABC News}}</ref> 

In the Senate, [[Cory Bernardi]]'s resignation in January 2020 allowed the Coalition to replace him with a Liberal member, increasing their share of seats in the Senate to 36.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bonyhady |first=Nick |title=Conservative senator Cory Bernardi resigns from Parliament |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/conservative-senator-cory-bernardi-resigns-from-parliament-20200120-p53t08.html |access-date=21 April 2022 |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |date=20 January 2020 |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421221929/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/conservative-senator-cory-bernardi-resigns-from-parliament-20200120-p53t08.html |url-status=live}}</ref> They retained this figure until [[Northern Territory]] senator [[Sam McMahon]] resigned from the [[Country Liberal Party]] in January 2022, four months before the election. She joined the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Australia)|Liberal Democratic Party]] on 8 April 2022.<ref>{{cite news |date=8 April 2022 |title=NT politician Sam McMahon joins ex-Queensland premier's party to run for re-election |publisher=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-08/sam-mcmahon-announces-federal-senate-election-bid-nt/100975390 |access-date=8 April 2022 |archive-date=11 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220411113907/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-08/sam-mcmahon-announces-federal-senate-election-bid-nt/100975390 |url-status=live|author=Garrick, Matt}}</ref>

In the House of Representatives, two Coalition MPs ([[Llew O'Brien]] and [[Darren Chester]]) departed their respective party-room caucuses, though retained their membership of the Morrison Government.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/chester-s-departure-might-be-least-of-joyce-s-troubles-right-now-20210926-p58uvg.html|title=Chester's departure might be least of Joyce's troubles right now|date=26 September 2021 |publisher=Sydney Morning Herald|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref> The government's share of seats in the House dropped when [[Craig Kelly]], the member for [[Division of Hughes|Hughes]], left the Liberal Party in August 2021 to become an independent and sit on the crossbench.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/feb/23/craig-kelly-quits-liberal-party-to-move-to-the-crossbench|title=Craig Kelly quits Liberal party to move to the crossbench|work=The Guardian|date=23 February 2021 |accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref> This left the government with a one-seat majority (76 out of 151), though considering the position of the [[Speaker of the Australian House of Representatives|Speaker]], who is obliged not to vote to create a majority where none is present, the government functioned from this point to the election in technical-minority status. On 7 April 2022, three days prior to the election being called, [[Liberal National Party of Queensland|Liberal National Party]] MP [[George Christensen]] announced his resignation from the party and became an independent, dropping the government to 75 seats at the end of the parliamentary term.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-01-19/george-christensen-resigns-chair-committee-covid-vaccine-comment/100767918|title=George Christensen steps down as chair of parliamentary committee after controversial COVID-19 vaccine comments|newspaper=ABC News |date=19 January 2022 |publisher=ABC|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref>

There were two [[by-elections]] in the 46th parliament, both in 2020 in the seats of [[2020 Eden-Monaro by-election|Eden-Monaro]] and [[2020 Groom by-election|Groom]]; in both instances, the by-elections were won by the incumbent party.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wheaton |first1=Claire |title=Eden-Monaro MP Mike Kelly resigns, prompting by-election |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-04-30/mike-kelly-eden-monaro-mp-resigns-triggering-by-election/12196936 |accessdate=30 April 2020 |work=ABC News |date=30 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Chen |first1=David |last2=Robinson |first2=Lucy |title=Federal LNP MP John McVeigh resigns from Groom electorate in Toowoomba |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-09-18/federal-member-for-groom-john-mcveigh-resigns-toowoomba/12676980 |access-date=18 September 2020 |work=ABC Southern Queensland |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |date=18 September 2020}}</ref> [[Nick Champion]] resigned from the House of Representatives in February 2022 to contest the [[2022 South Australian state election|South Australian state election]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://7news.com.au/politics/federal-labor-mp-nick-champion-resigns-c-5809219|title=Federal Labor MP Nick Champion resigns|date=23 February 2022 |publisher=7News|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref> A by-election was not held for his seat of [[Division of Spence|Spence]] as it would be too close to the federal election.<ref name=hor_29mar2022/>

===Events of the 46th Parliament===
Throughout the duration of the 46th Parliament, [[Scott Morrison]] remained Prime Minister and leader of the Liberal Party, and in so doing he became the first prime minister to serve a full term without facing a [[leadership spill]] since [[John Howard]] (1996–2007).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/careful-they-might-hear-you-why-scott-morrison-s-ministry-is-so-quiet-20211007-p58y75.html|title=Careful, they might hear you: Why Scott Morrison's ministry is so quiet|last=Crowe|first=David|date=7 October 2021|work=The Sydney Morning Herald|access-date=14 January 2022|archive-date=14 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220114064620/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/careful-they-might-hear-you-why-scott-morrison-s-ministry-is-so-quiet-20211007-p58y75.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Deputy Prime Minister and National Party leader [[Michael McCormack (Australian politician)|Michael McCormack]] was challenged twice by his predecessor [[Barnaby Joyce]], unsuccessfully in [[2020 National Party of Australia leadership spill|February 2020]] and successfully in [[2021 National Party of Australia leadership spill|June 2021]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/jun/21/barnaby-joyce-to-return-as-deputy-pm-after-deposing-michael-mccormack-as-nationals-leader|title=New Nationals leader Barnaby Joyce says he's a 'better person' and offers 'best chance of winning'|work=The Guardian|date=21 June 2021 |accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref>

Key events during the [[Second Morrison Ministry|second term]] and first full term of the [[Morrison Government]] included the [[Black Summer bushfires]], the ongoing [[COVID-19 pandemic in Australia|COVID-19 pandemic]], the [[2021 Australian Parliament House sexual misconduct allegations|Parliament House sexual misconduct allegations]], and the formation of the [[AUKUS]] security pact.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2021-03-25/lurid-sex-scandals-cost-australia-s-scott-morrison-his-covid-dividend|title=Lurid Sex Scandals Cost Australia's Scott Morrison His COVID Dividend|newspaper=Bloomberg.com |date=25 March 2021 |publisher=Bloomberg|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cfr.org/blog/could-aukus-deal-strengthen-deterrence-against-china-and-yet-come-real-cost-australia|title=Could the AUKUS Deal Strengthen Deterrence Against China—And Yet Come at a Real Cost to Australia?|publisher=CFR|accessdate=21 May 2022}}</ref> Morrison won praise for his response to the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, including launching the [[National Cabinet (Australia)|National Cabinet]] and [[JobKeeper]] programs, but he struggled to manage the [[COVID-19 vaccination in Australia|vaccination roll out]] and [[COVID-19 testing|testing regime]] as new [[variants of SARS-CoV-2]] emerged.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/jul/22/im-sorry-scott-morrison-apologises-for-slow-pace-of-australias-vaccine-rollout|title='I'm sorry': Scott Morrison apologises for slow pace of Australia's vaccine rollout|work=The Guardian|date=22 July 2021 |accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref> He faced further criticism for holidaying in Hawaii during the Black Summer bush fires, being accused of lying by French President [[Emmanuel Macron]] in the aftermath of the AUKUS agreement, and lacking ambition on climate change during [[2021 United Nations Climate Change Conference|COP26]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2019/dec/21/scott-morrison-hawaii-horror-show-pr-disaster-unfolded|title=Scott Morrison's Hawaii horror show: how a PR disaster unfolded|work=The Guardian|date=20 December 2019 |accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/11/03/world/australia/australia-scott-morrison-cop26.html|title=Australia's climate pledges fall short, again, critics say.|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=3 November 2021 |accessdate=22 May 2022|last1=Victor |first1=Daniel |last2=Cave |first2=Damien }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-11-02/why-macron-is-calling-scott-morrison-a-liar-over-submarines/100587732|title=Prime Minister Scott Morrison has been accused of lying to the French over the submarine deal. Here's what got us to this point|newspaper=ABC News |date=2 November 2021 |publisher=ABC|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref>

The opposition Labor Party elected [[Anthony Albanese]] as party leader unopposed, 12 days after [[Bill Shorten]] lost the May 2019 election. The [[Shadow Ministry of Anthony Albanese|Albanese-led Opposition]] struggled to make an impact in the early days of the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://theconversation.com/with-the-government-on-the-ropes-anthony-albanese-has-a-fighting-chance-158129|title=With the government on the ropes, Anthony Albanese has a fighting chance|last=Bongiorno|first=Frank|date=7 April 2021|work=The Conservation|access-date=14 January 2022|archive-date=14 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220114064615/https://theconversation.com/with-the-government-on-the-ropes-anthony-albanese-has-a-fighting-chance-158129|url-status=live}}</ref> His "most significant policy announcement" before 2022 was a commitment to reduce [[Greenhouse gas emissions by Australia|emissions]] by 43% by 2030 under a Labor government.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/dec/03/anthony-albanese-commits-labor-to-emissions-reduction-target-of-43-by-2030|title=Anthony Albanese commits Labor to emissions reduction target of 43% by 2030|last=Murphy|first=Katharine|website=[[The Guardian]]|date=3 December 2021|access-date=14 January 2022|archive-date=14 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220114064619/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/dec/03/anthony-albanese-commits-labor-to-emissions-reduction-target-of-43-by-2030|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Australian Greens]] leader [[Richard Di Natale]] resigned in February 2020, replaced by the party's only lower house MP [[Adam Bandt]], who was [[Australian Greens leadership elections#2020|elected unopposed]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-02-04/adam-bandt-elected-federal-greens-leader/11924970|title=Adam Bandt elected unopposed as federal Greens leader; Larissa Waters and Nick McKim as deputies|newspaper=ABC News |date=3 February 2020 |publisher=ABC|accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref> Among minor parties, controversial figure [[Craig Kelly]] resigned from the Liberal Party and became the leader of [[Clive Palmer]]'s [[United Australia Party (2013)|United Australia Party]] in 2021.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/sep/08/clive-palmer-and-craig-kelly-using-spam-text-messages-to-capture-rightwing-vote-ahead-of-election-expert-says|title=Clive Palmer and Craig Kelly using spam text messages to capture rightwing vote ahead of election, expert says|work=The Guardian|date=8 September 2021 |accessdate=22 May 2022}}</ref>

===Change in party registration rules===
{{see also|Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918#2021 amendments}}
In September 2021, legislation was passed to amend the ''[[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]'' and tighten rules surrounding the registration of political parties. Changes to party registration rules were reportedly the effect of an increase of parties on the Senate ballot, which resulted in the requirement of magnifying sheets for some voters to read the ballot, and a perception that voters would be misled by names of some minor parties.<ref name="partyregchange"/>

The first change was the increase of membership requirements for a party from 500 to 1,500.<ref name="partyregchange">{{cite news |title=Changes to federal election rules including party sizes and names pass Parliament |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-08-26/federal-election-rule-change-party-size-name-pass-parliament/100410134 |last=Lowrey |first=Tom |access-date=5 January 2022 |work=ABC News |date=26 August 2021 |archive-date=5 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220105101844/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-08-26/federal-election-rule-change-party-size-name-pass-parliament/100410134 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="partyregchange2">{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/guide/index.htm|title=Electoral Legislation Amendment (Party Registration Integrity) Act 2021 (Party Registration Act)|website=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328170346/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/guide/index.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> This resulted in the federal registration of non-parliamentary minor parties who could not prove they had at least 1,500 members, including the [[Christian Democratic Party (Australia)|Christian Democratic Party]] and [[Democratic Labour Party (Australia)|Democratic Labour Party]] in March 2022.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/parties_and_representatives/Party_Registration/Deregistered_parties/index.htm|title=Deregistered political parties|website=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=22 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322031621/https://www.aec.gov.au/parties_and_representatives/Party_Registration/Deregistered_parties/index.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

The second change was that parties cannot have names that were too similar to political parties registered before them. This meant that new parties are prevented from registering a party name and/or logo "too similar to an existing party's".<ref name="partyregchange"/><ref name="partyregchange2"/> As for existing registered parties, a party may also object to a similar name and/or logo used by another party, if the latter party was registered later than the former party. If the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] (AEC) is satisfied with the objection, it can uphold the objection, and the later-registered party will be registered within a month of the upholding, if an application to change the name and/or logo is not made or has been denied.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/legislation/bills/r6755_aspassed/toc_pdf/21101b01.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf|title=Electoral Legislation Amendment (Party Registration Integrity) Bill 2021 – As passed by both Houses|website=Parliament of Australia|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=28 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828094922/https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/legislation/bills/r6755_aspassed/toc_pdf/21101b01.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

This "similar name" rule was used by the [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]] against the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Australia)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) and [[The New Liberals]], with both objections upheld by the AEC. This forced The New Liberals to change its name to TNL to be registered and forced the LDP to apply to change its name to the Liberty and Democracy Party.<ref>{{cite web |title=Notice under s 133(1A)(a) of the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918 |url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2022/registration-approved-notice-tnl.pdf |website=Australian Electoral Commission |access-date=18 March 2022 |archive-date=25 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220325202947/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2022/registration-approved-notice-tnl.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> The LDP then withdrew its name change application on 22 March 2022.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/applications/files/2022/advertisement-change-of-name-liberal-democratic-party.pdf|title=Register of Political Parties – Liberal Democratic Party|website=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=27 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220327223340/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/applications/files/2022/advertisement-change-of-name-liberal-democratic-party.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2022/liberal-democratic-party-statement-of-reasons.pdf|title=Notice of Party Registration Decision – Application to Change the Register of Political Parties – Liberal Democratic Party|website=Australian Electoral Commission|date=1 April 2022|access-date=11 April 2022|archive-date=7 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220407122804/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2022/liberal-democratic-party-statement-of-reasons.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> As a result, on 1 April 2022, the AEC gave notice to the party that it would consider registering the latter, giving one month for the party to appeal the notice.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/applications/files/2022/web-notice-of-intention-to-deregister-liberal-democratic-party.pdf|title=Notice of intention to deregister – Liberal Democratic Party|website=Australian Electoral Commission|date=1 April 2022|access-date=11 April 2022|archive-date=8 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220408023739/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/applications/files/2022/web-notice-of-intention-to-deregister-liberal-democratic-party.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> However, as the writs for the election were issued the following week on 11 April, the party register then would be "frozen" and this meant the party was allowed to contest the election with its current name.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://antonygreen.com.au/loophole-allows-liberal-democrats-to-retain-party-name/|title=Loophole allows Liberal Democrats to Retain their Party Name|publisher=Antony Green's Election Blog|date=5 April 2022|access-date=12 April 2022|archive-date=11 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220411234329/https://antonygreen.com.au/loophole-allows-liberal-democrats-to-retain-party-name/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]] also used the "similar name" rule against the Democratic Labour Party and the objection was upheld by the AEC, but the latter party was eventually deregistered for not meeting the membership number requirement.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2021/aec-web-notice-democratic-labour-party.pdf|title=Notice Of Party Registration Decision Objection To Continued Use Of A Name And Abbreviation Australian Labor Party (ALP)'S Objection To Democratic Labour Party|website=Australian Electoral Commission|date=19 November 2021|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=20 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220320030156/https://aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Registration_Decisions/2021/aec-web-notice-democratic-labour-party.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Deregistered_parties/files/web-notice-to-deregister-democratic-labour-party.pdf|title=Notice of deregistration Democratic Labour Party|website=Australian Electoral Commission|date=4 March 2022|access-date=22 March 2022|archive-date=23 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220323161033/https://www.aec.gov.au/Parties_and_Representatives/Party_Registration/Deregistered_parties/files/web-notice-to-deregister-democratic-labour-party.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Party preselection issues===
{{see also|Australian Labor Party (Victorian Branch)|Liberal Party of Australia (New South Wales Division)}}
Both the Labor Party and Liberal Party experienced preselection issues, where they were unable to finalise candidates for many of the seats or the Senate as late as early April 2022, less than two weeks before the election was called. This resulted in the intervention by the parties' national executives or nominated committees to select the candidates and bypassing local voting by rank-and-file members. The New South Wales state division of the Liberal Party was unable to finalise candidates for many seats by March 2022 due to the alleged failure of Morrison's representative [[Alex Hawke]] to attend internal Liberal Party nomination review committee meetings and COVID-19 complications resulting in the inability to elect the state executive in November 2020.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-02-24/nsw-liberal-party-pre-selection-court-stoush/100857000|title=Court asked to rule in long-running Liberal Party power struggle over who gets to choose candidates in NSW seats|publisher=ABC News|date=24 February 2022|access-date=29 March 2022|archive-date=30 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330023355/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-02-24/nsw-liberal-party-pre-selection-court-stoush/100857000|url-status=live}}</ref> This had forced the federal executive of the party to temporarily dissolve the state executive on two occasions (4 to 8 March, and 27 March to 2 April) under the party constitution, and set up a committee to intervene in preselection processes. The committee was made up of Morrison, [[Premier of New South Wales|New South Wales Premier]] and state party leader [[Dominic Perrottet]], and former party president [[Chris McDiven]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://7news.com.au/politics/federal-liberals-weigh-up-nsw-candidates-c-5923144|title=Federal Liberals step in over NSW stoush|publisher=7News|date=4 March 2022|access-date=29 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328080347/https://7news.com.au/politics/federal-liberals-weigh-up-nsw-candidates-c-5923144|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/7645072/pm-takes-joint-control-of-nsw-liberals-can-choose-candidates/?cs=14264|title=Federal arm of Liberal Party takes over NSW branch for not complying with preselection rules|newspaper=The Canberra Times|date=4 March 2022|access-date=29 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328080336/https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/7645072/pm-takes-joint-control-of-nsw-liberals-can-choose-candidates/?cs=14264|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/7674988/federal-liberals-take-over-nsw-branch-again/|title=Scott Morrison, Dominic Perrottet and Christine McDiven take over NSW Liberals branch for second time in a month|newspaper=The Canberra Times|date=27 March 2022|access-date=29 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328081857/https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/7674988/federal-liberals-take-over-nsw-branch-again/|url-status=live}}</ref>

While the Liberal state executive was dissolved, the committee was allowed to "hand-pick" party candidates for the election and bypass local pre-selection ballots. It endorsed the preselection of Hawke, minister [[Sussan Ley]] and backbencher [[Trent Zimmerman]] in their seats on 6 March, and endorsed candidates on 2 April for nine key seats that the party was trying to win, including [[Division of Warringah|Warringah]], [[Division of Hughes|Hughes]], [[Division of Eden-Monaro|Eden-Monaro]], and [[Division of Parramatta|Parramatta]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://7news.com.au/politics/pm-avoids-minister-pre-selection-stoush-c-5980042|title=PM avoids minister pre-selection stoush|publisher=7News|date=8 March 2022|access-date=30 March 2022|archive-date=26 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220426084950/https://7news.com.au/politics/pm-avoids-minister-pre-selection-stoush-c-5980042|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-02/federal-liberals-intervene-to-make-nsw-preselections/100961474|title=Liberal federal executive intervenes to select candidates for nine NSW seats|publisher=ABC News|date=2 April 2022|access-date=4 April 2022|archive-date=3 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220403231646/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-02/federal-liberals-intervene-to-make-nsw-preselections/100961474|url-status=live}}</ref> Some party members sought to challenge the legitimacy of the committee's preselection in court, which would overturn the preselection of Hawke, Ley, Zimmerman and the other nine candidates.<ref name=30mar22>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/30/nsw-liberal-preselection-crisis-why-morrison-and-perrottet-want-to-rush-case-to-high-court|title=NSW Liberal preselection crisis: why Morrison and Perrottet want to rush case to high court|newspaper=The Guardian|date=30 March 2022|access-date=31 March 2022|archive-date=30 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330231010/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/30/nsw-liberal-preselection-crisis-why-morrison-and-perrottet-want-to-rush-case-to-high-court|url-status=live}}</ref> On 5 April, the [[New South Wales Court of Appeal]] ruled that the court had no jurisdiction to make decisions relating to the constitutions of political parties, thereby ruling the preselection of the 12 candidates valid.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-05/nsw-liberal-party-pre-selection-court-appeal-dismissed/100966516|title=Scott Morrison's court win as appeal against NSW Liberal pre-selections dismissed|publisher=ABC News|date=5 April 2022|access-date=5 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405050852/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-05/nsw-liberal-party-pre-selection-court-appeal-dismissed/100966516|url-status=live}}</ref> The legal challenge was further brought into [[High Court of Australia]] for appeal but was dismissed on 8 April, two days before the election was called.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-08/high-court-throws-out-liberal-preselection-matthew-camenzuli/100978564|title=High Court throws out challenge to NSW Liberal preselections, clearing way for Prime Minister to call federal election|publisher=ABC News|date=8 April 2022|access-date=11 April 2022|archive-date=11 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220411015711/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-08/high-court-throws-out-liberal-preselection-matthew-camenzuli/100978564|url-status=live}}</ref>

The preselection process in the Victorian branch of the Labor Party had been taken over by the [[Australian Labor Party National Executive|Labor Party National Executive]] in June 2020 until 2023 as a result of [[branch-stacking]] allegations within the party. Voting rights of all members were suspended and candidates would be chosen by the National Executive.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-06-17/labor-party-alp-pledges-to-clean-up-victorian-branch/12362744|title=Labor Party's national executive pledges to clean up troubled Victoria branch|website=ABC News|date=17 June 2020|access-date=17 June 2020|archive-date=17 June 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200617024720/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-06-17/labor-party-alp-pledges-to-clean-up-victorian-branch/12362744|url-status=live}}</ref> In early March 2022, the Labor Senate ticket for Victoria for the May federal election had still not yet been decided. It was reported that Senators [[Kimberley Kitching]] and [[Kim Carr]] might face preselection challenges and could lose preselection for the Senate ticket in the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/03/long-serving-labor-senator-kim-carr-set-to-lose-seat-in-preselection-challenge|title=Long-serving Labor senator Kim Carr set to lose seat in preselection challenge|work=The Guardian|date=3 March 2022|access-date=30 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328072248/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/03/long-serving-labor-senator-kim-carr-set-to-lose-seat-in-preselection-challenge|url-status=live|last=Martin|first=Sarah}}</ref> Kitching died from a heart attack a week later, and Carr later decided to retire from the election. On 28 March 2022, the National Executive was able to finalise two new candidates to replace Kitching and Carr, and another candidate for the [[Division of Holt]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://7news.com.au/politics/scramble-to-finalise-election-candidates-c-6228318|title=Labor finalise crucial election candidates|publisher=7News|date=28 March 2022|access-date=30 March 2022|archive-date=28 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328070309/https://7news.com.au/politics/scramble-to-finalise-election-candidates-c-6228318|url-status=live}}</ref>

Preselection issues in the Labor Party were not limited to the Victorian branch. On the same day as the replacements for Kitching and Carr were finalised, the National Executive "[[Parachute candidate|parachuted]]" [[Andrew Charlton (politician)|Andrew Charlton]] into the [[Division of Parramatta]] in New South Wales, bypassing a local preselection with three candidates from diverse ethnic backgrounds. This prompted a backlash from local party members and the incumbent retiring Labor member for Parramatta [[Julie Owens]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/29/disillusionment-grows-within-labor-about-the-partys-commitment-to-cultural-diversity|title=Disillusionment grows within Labor about the party's commitment to cultural diversity|newspaper=The Guardian|date=29 March 2022|access-date=30 March 2022|archive-date=30 March 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330004515/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/mar/29/disillusionment-grows-within-labor-about-the-partys-commitment-to-cultural-diversity|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Independents===
In the [[2013 Australian federal election|2013 federal election]], the [[Voices for Indi]] organisation successfully backed independent candidate [[Cathy McGowan (politician)|Cathy McGowan]] to defeat the incumbent Liberal member of parliament [[Sophie Mirabella]] in the [[Division of Indi]]. McGowan was reelected in 2016, retiring after two terms to be succeeded by fellow independent [[Helen Haines]]. McGowan's victory inspired the campaign of independent [[Zali Steggall]] in [[2019 Australian federal election|2019]], who defeated the Liberal former Prime Minister [[Tony Abbott]] in the seat of [[Division of Warringah|Warringah]]. In addition to Haines and Steggall's campaigns for reelection, the 2022 election saw the candidacy of several challengers who were in turn inspired by Steggall.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/19/world/australia/federal-election-independents.html|title=How a Group of Female Independents Aims to Revive Australian Democracy|work=The New York Times|date=19 May 2022 |accessdate=22 May 2022|last1=Cave |first1=Damien }}</ref> Termed "[[teal independents]]" (denoting a mix of [[classical liberal]] blue and [[environmentalist]] green), these candidates contested in Liberal heartlands notably including [[Division of Goldstein|Goldstein]], [[Division of Kooyong|Kooyong]], [[Division of Mackellar|Mackellar]], and [[Division of Wentworth|Wentworth]]. Each received funds from the political fundraising group [[Climate 200]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Millar |first1=Royce |title=A secret party? Immoral? Explaining who the 'teal' independents really are |url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/a-secret-party-immoral-explaining-who-the-teal-independents-really-are-20220505-p5aio4.html |access-date=6 May 2022 |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |date=6 May 2022}}</ref>

== State of electorates ==

===Redistribution===
{{see also|Divisions of the Australian House of Representatives|Redistribution (Australia)}}
The [[Australian Electoral Commission]] is required, one year after the first sitting day for a new House of Representatives, to determine the number of members to which each State and Territory is entitled. If the number in any state changes, a redistribution will be required in those states. A redistribution will be postponed if it would begin within one year of the expiration of the House of Representatives. Demographic statistics for December 2019 released by the [[Australian Bureau of Statistics]] on 18 June 2020 were used to calculate the determination. The population counts confirmed that the number of seats in the House of Representatives was to return to 150, with Victoria gaining a seat (39), and Western Australia (15) and the Northern Territory (1) losing a seat each.<ref name="repent">{{cite web|last = Green|first = Antony|author-link = Antony Green|title = ABS Population Statistics Confirm Changes in House Representation|url = https://antonygreen.com.au/abs-population-statistics-confirm-changes-in-house-representation/|website = Antony Green's Election Blog|access-date = 18 June 2020|date = 18 June 2020|archive-date = 19 June 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200619203059/https://antonygreen.com.au/abs-population-statistics-confirm-changes-in-house-representation/|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|publisher = [[Australian Electoral Commission]]|url = https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2020/07-03.htm|date = 3 July 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|title = Determination of membership entitlement to the House of Representatives|type = Media Release|archive-date = 29 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210329090727/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2020/07-03.htm|url-status = live}}</ref>

The abolition of the Northern Territory's second seat in the determination was controversial.<ref>{{cite news|title = Calls for Federal Government to save second NT lower house seat axed in redistribution|first = Jacqueline|last = Breen|date = 3 July 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|work = [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|url = https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-07-03/nt-calls-to-save-second-lower-house-seat-axed/12420032|archive-date = 30 September 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210930225259/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-07-03/nt-calls-to-save-second-lower-house-seat-axed/12420032|url-status = live}}</ref> Labor Party senators [[Malarndirri McCarthy]] and [[Don Farrell]] put forward a [[private senator's bill]] which would guarantee the Northern Territory a minimum two seats in the House of Representatives, with the bill referred to the Joint Standing Committee on Electoral Matters.<ref name = ParlL-Muller/> In July 2020, election analyst [[Antony Green]] proposed to the Joint Standing Committee on Electoral Matters that the "[[harmonic mean]] method" be used to calculate the electoral representation entitlements for the territories.<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.aph.gov.au/DocumentStore.ashx?id=1b4cbf9e-6763-437b-bf78-78626b6ad8de&subId=686254|last = Green|first = Antony|author-link = Antony Green|title = Fair Representation for the Territories – A Submission to the Joint Standing Committee of Electoral Matters|website = [[Parliament of Australia]]|date = July 2020|access-date = 11 December 2020|archive-date = 5 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210305141615/https://www.aph.gov.au/DocumentStore.ashx?id=1b4cbf9e-6763-437b-bf78-78626b6ad8de&subId=686254|url-status = live}}</ref> Green also blogged on the history of representation and its applications to states and territories in light of the 2020 redistribution<ref>{{cite web|last = Green|first = Antony|author-link = Antony Green|url = https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-1-allocating-to-the-states/|title = 2020 Apportionment of Seats: Part 1 – Allocating to the States|website = Antony Green's Election Blog|date = 8 June 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 18 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210318045600/https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-1-allocating-to-the-states/|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last = Green|author-link = Antony Green|first = Antony|url = https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-2-allocating-to-the-territories/|title = 2020 Apportionment of Seats: Part 2 – Allocating to the Territories|website = Antony Green's Election Blog|date = 9 June 2020|access-date = 11 December 2020|archive-date = 7 December 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201207150350/https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-2-allocating-to-the-territories/|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last = Green|first = Antony|author-link = Antony Green|url = https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-3-changing-the-formula-for-states/|title = 2020 Apportionment of Seats: Part 3 – Changing the Formula for States|website = Antony Green's Election Blog|date = 17 June 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 18 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210318045726/https://antonygreen.com.au/2020-apportionment-of-seats-part-3-changing-the-formula-for-states/|url-status = live}}</ref> and his advocacy proved persuasive.<ref name=ParlL-Muller/> In October 2020, deputy prime minister [[Michael McCormack (Australian politician)|Michael McCormack]] gave an assurance that the government and opposition would combine to overrule the AEC and maintain the Northern Territory's level of representation. The mechanism by which this would be used to achieved was unclear,<ref name="ntseats">{{cite news|title = Second Lower House NT seat saved as Labor, Coalition unite to overturn AEC change|first = Cameron|last = Gooley|url = https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-10-08/nt-coalition-support-second-northern-territory-political-seat/12743626|access-date = 2 April 2021|work = [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date = 8 October 2020|archive-date = 1 April 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210401081230/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-10-08/nt-coalition-support-second-northern-territory-political-seat/12743626|url-status = live}}</ref> however, with Senator [[Mathias Cormann]] stating that a two-seat minimum for the territories would be legislated.<ref>{{cite news|first = Doug|last = Dingwall|work = [[The Canberra Times]]|title = Support for ACT, NT to have two-seat guarantee in lower house|url = https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/6972488/support-for-act-nt-to-have-two-seat-guarantee-in-lower-house/|date = 17 October 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 24 October 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201024115742/https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/6972488/support-for-act-nt-to-have-two-seat-guarantee-in-lower-house/|url-status = live}}</ref> Mandating a minimum number of seats for the Northern Territory but not the Australian Capital Territory was seen as potentially inequitable, though the ACT's level of representation was not under threat.<ref name = ParlL-Muller /> A 2003 report had also recommended against adopting mandatory minimum entitlements to seats in the House of Representatives for either of the territories.<ref>{{cite book|date = November 2003|title = Report of the Inquiry into increasing the minimum representation of the Australian Capital Territory and the Northern Territory in the House of Representatives|publisher = [[Parliament of Australia]]|url = https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/House_of_Representatives_Committees?url=em/territories/report/fullreport.pdf|author = Joint Standing Committee on Electoral Matters|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 27 August 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210827070303/https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/House_of_Representatives_Committees?url=em%2Fterritories%2Freport%2Ffullreport.pdf|url-status = live}}</ref>

Ultimately, the Joint Standing Committee recommended "enacting a harmonic mean for allocating seats between states and territories, with appropriate public explanation to build understanding for the reform".<ref name = ParlL-Muller/> The Parliament passed the ''Electoral Amendment (Territory Representation) Act'' on 9 December 2020, amending the ''[[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]'' to use the harmonic mean method for determining representative entitlements for territories relative to states.<ref name=ntseatsfinal>{{cite web|url = https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-12-10/nt-keeps-two-federal-seats-after-electoral-amendment-bill-passes/12969366|title = NT to keep two federal House of Representative seats at next election after legislation passes Parliament|work = [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date = 9 December 2020|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 30 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210330224133/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-12-10/nt-keeps-two-federal-seats-after-electoral-amendment-bill-passes/12969366|url-status = live}}</ref><ref name="billsenate">{{cite news|title = 'A shame job': Bill to safeguard two federal members for the NT a step closer to reality|url = https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-12-03/bill-to-secure-two-nt-federal-members-passes-senate/12941366|access-date = 9 December 2020|work = [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date = 3 December 2020|archive-date = 8 December 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201208120749/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2020-12-03/bill-to-secure-two-nt-federal-members-passes-senate/12941366|url-status = live|author=Gooley, Cameron}}</ref> Consequently, the Northern Territory will retain two seats in the House of Representatives at the next election,<ref name=ntseatsfinal/> an outcome achieved without legislating any mandatory minimum level of representation.<ref name = ParlL-Muller>{{cite web|last = Muller|first = Damon|date = 2 December 2020|access-date = 11 December 2020|title = Electoral Amendment (Territory Representation) Bill 2020|publisher = [[Parliamentary Library of Australia]], [[Parliament of Australia]]|url = https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/FlagPost/2020/December/Electoral_Amendment_Territory_Representation_Bill_2020|archive-date = 10 December 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201210034653/https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Parliamentary_Departments/Parliamentary_Library/FlagPost/2020/December/Electoral_Amendment_Territory_Representation_Bill_2020|url-status = live}}</ref>

