In March 2021, the AEC published its proposal for this redistribution, involving the abolition of the [[Division of Stirling]] in Western Australia,<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/wa/proposed-redistribution/files/Proposed-redistribution-of-WA-March-2021.pdf|title = Proposed redistribution of Western Australia into electoral divisions|date = March 2021|publisher = [[Australian Electoral Commission]]|author = Redistribution Committee for Western Australia|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 8 April 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210408163608/https://aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/wa/proposed-redistribution/files/Proposed-redistribution-of-WA-March-2021.pdf|url-status = live}}</ref> the creation of the new [[Division of Hawke]] in Victoria (named for former Prime Minister [[Bob Hawke]]), and the renaming of the existing [[Division of Corangamite]] to the Division of Tucker (in honour of [[Margaret Tucker]], "a [[Yorta Yorta people|Yorta Yorta woman]], for her significant work to create a more equal and understanding society for Aboriginal people").<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/vic/proposed-redistribution/files/Proposed-redistribution-of-Victoria-March-2021.pdf|title = Proposed redistribution of Victoria into electoral divisions|date = March 2021|publisher = [[Australian Electoral Commission]]|author = Redistribution Committee for Victoria|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 4 April 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210404104906/https://aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/vic/proposed-redistribution/files/Proposed-redistribution-of-Victoria-March-2021.pdf|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title = Proposed federal electoral divisions for Victoria released|type = Media Release|publisher = [[Australian Electoral Commission]]|url = https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2021/03-19.htm|date = 19 March 2021|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 26 November 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20211126030639/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2021/03-19.htm|url-status = live}}</ref> When the AEC published its final determinations in June 2021, the abolition of Stirling<ref name="waredist">{{cite web |title=Announcement of names and boundaries of federal electoral divisions in Western Australia |url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/wa/announce-names-boundaries.htm |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |access-date=29 June 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613141029/https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/wa/announce-names-boundaries.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> and creation of Hawke were confirmed,<ref name="vicredist">{{cite web |title=Announcement of final boundaries – Victorian federal redistribution |url=https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/vic/announce-names-boundaries.htm |publisher=Australian Electoral Commission |access-date=29 June 2021 |archive-date=29 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210629053314/https://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/2021/vic/announce-names-boundaries.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> but Corangamite would not be renamed to Tucker over concerns that it would be vandalised as "Fucker".<ref name="tucker">{{cite news |last1=Harris |first1=Rob |title=Graffiti fears rule out renaming electorate 'Tucker' |url=https://www.theage.com.au/politics/federal/graffiti-fears-rule-out-renaming-electorate-tucker-20210629-p585cq.html |access-date=29 June 2021 |work=The Age |date=29 June 2021 |archive-date=29 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210629093119/https://www.theage.com.au/politics/federal/graffiti-fears-rule-out-renaming-electorate-tucker-20210629-p585cq.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Election pendulum (House of Representatives)===
{{main|Pre-election pendulum for the 2022 Australian federal election}}

==Voter registration==
Enrollment of eligible voters is compulsory. Voters must notify the AEC within 8 weeks of a change of address or after turning 18. The electoral rolls are closed for new enrollments or update of details about a week after the issue of [[Writ of election#Australia|writs for election]].<ref name=CEAs155>{{cite web|url = http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s155.html|work = [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]|title = Section 155: Date for close of Rolls|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 31 July 2017|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170731082910/http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s155.html|url-status = live}}</ref> Enrollment is optional for 16- or 17-year-olds, but they cannot vote until they turn 18,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/enrol/|title=Enrol to vote|website=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=23 April 2021|archive-date=1 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191001230634/https://www.aec.gov.au/enrol/|url-status=live}}</ref> and persons who have applied for Australian citizenship may also apply for provisional enrollment which takes effect on the granting of citizenship.<ref>Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918, s. 99A.</ref> A total of 17,228,900 people were enrolled to vote in the election, which meant that 96.8% of all eligible Australians were enrolled on the electoral roll.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/04-21.htm|title=Final enrolment numbers now available|work=aec.gov.au|date=21 April 2022|access-date=21 April 2022|archive-date=21 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421080303/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/04-21.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Election date==
{{see also|Electoral system of Australia#Election day}}
The constitutional and legal provisions which impact on the choice of election dates include:<ref>{{cite web|last = Lundie|first = Rob|date = 25 June 2009|url = http://www.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/BN/2008-09/aust_elections.htm|title = Australian elections timetable|publisher = [[Parliamentary Library of Australia]], [[Parliament of Australia]]|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110108212031/http://www.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/bn/2008-09/Aust_elections.htm|archive-date = 8 January 2011|url-status = dead}}</ref><ref name=green_date>{{cite web|last1=Riga|first1=Jessica|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-10/federal-election-live-may-21-scott-morrison/100960362/|title=Prime Minister Scott Morrison announces May 21 election|publisher=ABC|date=10 April 2022|access-date=9 April 2022|archive-date=9 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220409231541/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-10/federal-election-live-may-21-scott-morrison/100960362|url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[Section 12 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 12 of the Constitution]] says: "The Governor of any State may cause writs to be issued for the election of Senators for that State."<ref name = CoAs12>{{cite web|url = http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s12.html|title = Section 12: Issue of writs|work = [[Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act]] 1900|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 29 February 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200229071616/http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s12.html|url-status = live}}</ref>
* [[Section 13 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 13 of the Constitution]] provides that the election of senators shall be held in the period of twelve months before the places become vacant.<ref name = CoAs13>{{cite web|work = [[Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act]] 1900|url = http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s13.html|title = Section 13: Rotation of Senators|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 17 April 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210417235003/http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s13.html|url-status = live}}</ref>
* [[Section 28 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 28 of the Constitution]] says: "Every House of Representatives shall continue for three years from the first sitting of the House, and no longer, but may be sooner dissolved by the Governor-General."<ref name=CoAs28>{{cite web|url = http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s28.html|work = [[Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act]] 1900|title = Section 28: Duration of House of Representatives|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 18 August 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210818032731/http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s28.html|url-status = live}}</ref> Since the 46th Parliament of Australia opened on 2 July 2019, it will expire on 1 July 2022.
* [[Section 32 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 32 of the Constitution]] says: "The writs shall be issued within ten days from the expiry of a House of Representatives or from the proclamation of a dissolution thereof."<ref name=CoAs32>{{cite web|url = http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s32.html|work = [[Commonwealth of Australia Constitution Act]] 1900|title = Section 32: Writs for general election|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 18 August 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210818042758/http://classic.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s32.html|url-status = live}}</ref> Ten days after 1 July 2022 is 11 July 2022.
* Section 156(1) of the CEA says: "The date fixed for the nomination of the candidates shall not be less than 10 days nor more than 27 days after the date of the writ."<ref name=CEAs156>{{cite web|url = http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s156.html|work = [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]|title = Section 156: Date of nomination|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 4 March 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160304095625/http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s156.html|url-status = live}}</ref> Twenty-seven days after 11 July 2022 is 7 August 2022.
* Section 157 of the CEA says: "The date fixed for the polling shall not be less than 23 days nor more than 31 days after the date of nomination."<ref name=CEAs157>{{cite web|work = [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]|title = Section 157: Date of polling|url = http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s157.html|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 31 July 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160731042725/http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s157.html|url-status = live}}</ref> Thirty-one days after 7 August 2022 is 7 September 2022, a Wednesday.
* Section 158 of the CEA says: "The day fixed for the polling shall be a Saturday."<ref name=CEAs158>{{cite web|work = [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]]|title = Section 158: Polling to be on a Saturday|url = http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s158.html|publisher = [[Australasian Legal Information Institute]] (AustLII)|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 4 March 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160304045328/http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s158.html|url-status = live}}</ref> The Saturday before 7 September 2022 is 3 September 2022, which was the latest possible date for the lower house election.<ref>{{cite web|last=Green|first=Antony|date=6 March 2022|url=https://antonygreen.com.au/why-the-2022-house-and-senate-elections-will-be-held-on-the-same-day/|title=Why the 2022 House and Senate Elections will be held on the same day|website=Antony Green's Election Blog|access-date=25 May 2022}}</ref>

===Dissolution of parliament===
The election was called by Morrison on 10 April 2022, when he visited the [[Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]] advising the latter to prorogue Parliament and dissolve the House of Representatives. The Governor-General accepted Morrison's recommendations, as is the custom in Australia's Westminster system of government.<ref name="timing101">{{cite web|url=https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-04/20220410%20Documents%20relating%20to%20the%20calling%20of%20the%202022%20Federal%20Election.pdf|title=Documents relating to the calling of the 2022 Federal Election|work=Governor-General of Australia|date=10 April 2022|access-date=11 April 2022|archive-date=11 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220411003642/https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-04/20220410%20Documents%20relating%20to%20the%20calling%20of%20the%202022%20Federal%20Election.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.legislation.gov.au/Details/C2022G00298|title=Proclamation – Prorogue the Parliament and dissolve the House of Representatives – 11 April 2022|work=Federal Register of Legislation|publisher=Australian Government|date=11 April 2022|access-date=12 April 2022|archive-date=26 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220426084950/https://www.legislation.gov.au/Details/C2022G00298|url-status=live}}</ref> The Parliament was then prorogued and the House of Representatives dissolved the next morning.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/House_of_Representatives/About_the_House_News/News/The_Parliament_has_been_prorogued_and_dissolved_but_what_does_this_mean|title=The Parliament has been prorogued and dissolved, but what does this mean?|publisher=Parliament of Australia|date=11 April 2022|access-date=25 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.timebase.com.au/news/2022/AT05327-article.html|title=What happens to Bills being considered in Commonwealth Parliament when an election is called?|website=TimeBase|date=6 May 2022|access-date=25 May 2022}}</ref>

==Election timeline==
On 10 April 2022, the office of the Governor-General released documents relating to the calling of the election. The documents set out a timeline of key dates for the election.<ref name=timing101/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://aec.gov.au/election/|title=2022 federal election Saturday 21 May 2022|website=Australian Electoral Commission|access-date=14 April 2022|archive-date=12 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220412105534/https://aec.gov.au/election/|url-status=live}}</ref>
* 11 April – 9:29&nbsp;am: Prorogation of the 46th Parliament
* 11 April – 9:30&nbsp;am: Dissolution of the House of Representatives
* 11 April – Issue of writs
* 18 April – Close of electoral rolls
* 21 April – Close of candidate nominations
* 22 April – Declaration of nominations
* 9 May – Early voting commences
* 18 May – Close of postal vote applications
* 21 May – Polling day; commencement of terms for territory senators
* 13 June – Last day for receipt of declaration votes
* 28 June – Return of writs (last day)
* 1 July – Commencement of terms for state senators

The election period included three national [[public holidays in Australia|public holidays]]: [[Good Friday]] (15 April), [[Easter Monday]] (18 April), and [[Anzac Day]] (25 April), as well as [[International Workers' Day|May Day]] and [[International Workers' Day|Labour Day]] in [[Northern Territory]] and [[Queensland]], respectively, both falling on 2 May.

