The first leaders' debate was held in Brisbane in front of 100 undecided voters. Moderated by Sky News reporter Kieran Gilbert, Albanese was declared the winner, with 40 votes to Morrison's 35 and 25 still undecided.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.news.com.au/national/federal-election/who-won-the-leaders-debate-scott-morrison-or-anthony-albanese/news-story/12a8071c14ed43d4526945194a928210|title=Who won the leaders debate? Scott Morrison or Anthony Albanese|date=20 April 2022|publisher=news.com.au|access-date=23 April 2022|archive-date=26 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220426084950/https://www.news.com.au/national/federal-election/who-won-the-leaders-debate-scott-morrison-or-anthony-albanese/news-story/12a8071c14ed43d4526945194a928210|url-status=live}}</ref> The first debate had 415,000 viewers.<ref name="auto"/>

The second leaders' debate was held at the Nine Studios in Sydney on 8 May. The debate was moderated by ''[[60 Minutes (Australian TV program)|60 Minutes]]'' journalist, Sarah Abo, with [[Nine Network|Channel 9]] political editor [[Chris Uhlmann]], [[The Sydney Morning Herald|Sydney Morning Herald]] chief political correspondent David Crowe and radio host Deb Knight asking questions of the leaders. The debate was broadcast nationwide on the [[Nine Network]]'s main [[free-to-air]] channel, the network's streaming service 9Now, and the websites of the newspapers owned by the network: ''[[The Age]]'' and ''[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]''.<ref name="auto"/> The winner of the debate was to be decided through a viewer poll hosted on Channel 9's website. Although 49% of viewers preferred Albanese to be the better prime minister compared to 45% preferring Morrison, the debate was a 50–50 draw.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-05-08/scott-morrison-anthony-albanese-second-leaders-debate/101048046#live-blog-post-1211130143|title=Federal election: Scott Morrison and Anthony Albanese face off in second leaders' debate — as it happened|work=ABC News|first1=Jessica|last1=Riga|first2=Georgia|last2=Hitch|date=8 May 2022|accessdate=8 May 2022}}</ref> Channel 9's moderation of the debate was subject to widespread criticism, with both Morrison and Albanese shouting over the top of one another and the moderator, and for the technical issues experienced by a web page run by Channel 9 to gather audience opinion. It was a ratings success, drawing in 641,000 viewers.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/09/second-leaders-debate-a-ratings-winner-for-nine-despite-employees-criticising-it-as-a-shambles|title=Second leaders' debate a ratings winner for Nine despite employees criticising it as a 'shambles'=The Guardian|first1=Elias|last1=Visontay|first2=Amanda|last2=Meade| website=[[TheGuardian.com]] |date=9 May 2022|accessdate=9 May 2022}}</ref>

The third and final leaders' debate was held on 11 May on [[Seven Network|Channel Seven]], whose political editor [[Mark Riley (journalist)|Mark Riley]] moderated the debate. To determine the winner of the debate, 150 undecided voters were surveyed in key electorates around the country. Albanese was victorious with 50% of the vote, with Morrison getting 34% of the vote, and 16% remaining undecided.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/11/albanese-wins-final-election-debate-over-morrison-according-to-sevens-undecided-voters|title=Albanese wins final election debate over Morrison, according to Seven's undecided voters|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Sarah|last=Martin|date=11 May 2022|accessdate=17 May 2022}}</ref> Seven's debate was viewed by 811,000 people, the highest viewership of all three debates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvblackbox.com.au/page/2022/05/12/sevens-the-great-debate-wins-its-timeslot-all-people-and-in-all-key-demos/|title=Final GREAT DEBATE delivers strong late night audience figures for Channel 7|work=TV Blackbox|first=Kevin|last=Perry|date=12 May 2022|accessdate=13 May 2022}}</ref>

The [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] (ABC) pushed for a debate on their free-to-air channel, radio, and websites in the lead-up to polling day, which Morrison refused, as well as Liberal Party federal director Andrew Hirst, who gave no explanation. Morrison and Albanese accepted a debate on Channel Seven rather than on the ABC.<ref>{{cite news|last=Meade|first=Amanda|date=3 May 2022|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/04/sevens-election-debate-to-air-after-big-brother-as-prime-minister-snubs-national-broadcaster|title=Seven's election debate to air after Big Brother as prime minister snubs ABC|newspaper=The Guardian|access-date=24 May 2022}}</ref>

===Campaign===
*'''11 April''': Labor Opposition Leader [[Anthony Albanese]] was unable to state the cash or unemployment rates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-11/albanese-cash-unemployment-rate-federal-election/100981834|title='When I make a mistake, I'll 'fess up to it': Albanese apologises after early economic stumble|date=11 April 2022|publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation|access-date=16 April 2022|archive-date=16 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220416032258/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-11/albanese-cash-unemployment-rate-federal-election/100981834|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/i-m-not-sure-what-it-is-anthony-albanese-stumbles-on-unemployment-rate-and-cash-rate-20220411-p5aci2.html|title='I made a mistake': Albanese stumbles on unemployment rate and cash rate|first=Lisa Visentin, Shane Wright, Katina|last=Curtis|date=11 April 2022|website=The Sydney Morning Herald|access-date=16 April 2022|archive-date=16 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220416032259/https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/i-m-not-sure-what-it-is-anthony-albanese-stumbles-on-unemployment-rate-and-cash-rate-20220411-p5aci2.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
