===Preferences===

Although ballot preferences are free for voters to decide, political parties usually make agreements as to how they would instruct their voters to rank candidates from other political parties—candidates or political parties that are ranked earlier numerically are said to be "preferenced"—through a "how-to-vote card" distributed by campaign volunteers.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Motherwell |first=Sarah |date=22 April 2022 |title=How does preferential voting work in Australia? |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-04-21/how-to-preference-voting-australia-federal-election/100991154 |access-date=29 April 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Rushforth |first=Charles |date=29 April 2022 |title=Confused About Preferences In Voting? Here's What You Need To Know |work=[[Junkee]] |url=https://junkee.com/preferential-voting-explainer/328886 |access-date=29 April 2022}}</ref> [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation]] said it would recommend that voters direct their preferences to Labor in five seats—[[Division of North Sydney|North Sydney]], [[Division of Goldstein|Goldstein]], [[Division of Sturt|Sturt]], [[Division of Longman|Longman]], and [[Division of Bass|Bass]]—all held by moderate Liberals.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/apr/28/one-nation-directs-preferences-to-labor-in-five-seats-targeting-left-leaning-liberals|title=One Nation directs preferences to Labor in five seats targeting 'left-leaning Liberals'|date=28 April 2022|access-date=3 May 2022|website=The Guardian}}</ref> 

The United Australia Party recommended its voters direct their preferences to the Coalition ahead of Labor in marginal electorates, such as [[Division of Bass|Bass]], [[Division of Chisholm|Chisholm]], [[Division of Dobell|Dobell]], [[Division of Gilmore|Gilmore]], [[Division of Hunter|Hunter]] and [[Division of Macquarie|Macquarie]], as well as all but four seats in Queensland, in addition to preferencing Liberal incumbents ahead of independent challengers in [[Division of Mackellar|Mackellar]], [[Division of Wentworth|Wentworth]], and [[Division of Wannon|Wannon]]. They also recommended its voters put incumbents last in their vote in Western Australia.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Martin |first1=Sarah |last2=Murphy |first2=Katharine |date=9 May 2022 |title=United Australia party directs preferences to Coalition in key marginal seats |work=[[Guardian Australia]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/09/united-australia-party-directs-preferences-to-coalition-in-key-marginal-seats |url-status=live |access-date=10 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220510015418/https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/may/09/united-australia-party-directs-preferences-to-coalition-in-key-marginal-seats |archive-date=10 May 2022}}</ref>

The [[Liberal National Party of Queensland]] recommended that those who vote for its candidates direct their preferences to One Nation in the Senate and key Queensland seats.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/apr/29/election-preference-deals-and-strategies-that-could-benefit-pauline-hanson-and-one-nation|title=Election preference deals and strategies that could benefit Pauline Hanson and One Nation|date=29 April 2022|website=The Guardian|author=Smee, Ben}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.brisbanetimes.com.au/politics/queensland/just-politics-lnp-urges-queensland-voters-to-preference-one-nation-20220428-p5agxr.html|title='Just politics': LNP urges Queensland voters to preference One Nation|first=Matt|last=Dennien|date=28 April 2022|website=Brisbane Times}}</ref> The [[Australian Greens|Greens]] recommended its voters direct their preferences to Labor ahead of both the [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] and minor right-wing parties such as the United Australia Party and One Nation for the House of Representatives and Senate, with preferences also recommended to be directed to independents endorsed by the various [[Voices groups in Australia|Voices groups]] in Liberal-held seats such as [[Division of Goldstein|Goldstein]], [[Division of Mackellar|Mackellar]], [[Division of North Sydney|North Sydney]], and [[Division of Wentworth|Wentworth]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=McIlroy |first=Tom |date=30 April 2022 |title=Greens to preference Labor ahead of Liberals around the country |work=[[Australian Financial Review]] |url=https://www.afr.com/politics/federal/greens-to-preference-labor-ahead-of-liberals-around-the-country-20220429-p5ah3m |url-status=live |access-date=30 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220430095538/https://www.afr.com/politics/federal/greens-to-preference-labor-ahead-of-liberals-around-the-country-20220429-p5ah3m |archive-date=30 April 2022}}</ref>

==Candidates==
{{Main|Candidates of the 2022 Australian federal election}}
[[File:Candidates at ECLC Chisholm federal election candidates forum (2) — May 2022.jpg|thumb|Some of the candidates for the [[Division of Chisholm]] at a candidates' forum]]
[[File:Corflutes for candidates at a polling station in Wanniassa - May 2022.jpg|thumb|Corflutes and banners for candidates at a polling station in Canberra]]
Candidates for either house must have been formally nominated with the Electoral Commission. The nomination for a party-endorsed candidate must be signed by the Registered Officer of a party registered under the Electoral Act; fifty signatures of eligible voters are required for an independent candidate. A candidate can nominate for only one electorate, and must pass a number of qualifications. ''The Electoral Legislation Amendment (Modernisation and Other Measures) Act 2019'' came into effect on 1 March 2019. A deposit of $2,000 was required for a candidate for the House of Representatives or the Senate, which is refunded if the candidate is elected or gains at least 4% of the first preference vote.<ref>{{cite book|publisher = [[Australian Electoral Commission]]|isbn = 9781921427367|title = Candidates Handbook – Federal elections and by-elections (Version 8)|date = 2 October 2020|url = https://www.aec.gov.au/elections/candidates/files/candidates-handbook.pdf|page = 6|access-date = 2 April 2021|archive-date = 29 March 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210329071820/https://aec.gov.au/elections/candidates/files/candidates-handbook.pdf|url-status = live}}</ref> Between 10 and 27 days must be allowed after the issue of writs before the close of nominations.<ref name="CEAs156"/> At the close of nominations a total of 1,624 candidates had stood for election, of which 1,203 were House of Representatives candidates and 421 were Senate candidates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/04-22a.htm|title=1,624 candidates announced as election operation ramps up|work=aec.gov.au|date=22 April 2022|access-date=22 April 2022|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422112603/https://www.aec.gov.au/media/2022/04-22a.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

The 2022 election featured the largest number of [[Aboriginal Australians|Indigenous]] candidates in Australian history, with four running for the Coalition, eleven for Labor, and seventeen for the Greens.<ref>{{cite web |last=Collard |first=Sarah |date=5 May 2022 |title=Meet the Indigenous candidates vying for your vote |url=https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/2022/05/05/meet-indigenous-candidates-vying-your-vote |access-date=7 May 2022 |publisher=NITV}}</ref>

