== Retiring members ==
<!-- This list is transcluded in [[Candidates of the next Australian federal election]]. Any mis-edits will disrupt the syntax of the tables in the latter. -->
<section begin="retiringmembers"/>
The seat of [[Division of Spence|Spence]] (SA) was vacant following the resignation of [[Nick Champion]] (Labor) on 22 February 2022 to contest the [[2022 South Australian state election|South Australian state election]]. A Senate seat in New South Wales was vacant following the resignation of [[Kristina Keneally]] (Labor) on 11 April 2022 to contest the lower house seat of [[Division of Fowler|Fowler]] in the election. A second Senate seat in Western Australia was initially vacant at the close of nominations following the resignation on 15 April 2022 of [[Ben Small (politician)|Ben Small]] (Liberal), who had discovered that he was ineligible on the grounds of dual citizenship. Having renounced his New Zealand citizenship, Small was re-appointed on 18 May 2022 and contested the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.parliament.wa.gov.au/Hansard/hansard.nsf/0/8961F86102ECD56F4825884B001347DC/$File/J41%20S1%2020220518%20All.pdf|title=Parliamentary Debates (Hansard) – Forty-First Parliament First Session 2022 – Joint Sitting Of The Legislative Council And Legislative Assembly – Wednesday, 18 May 2022|publisher=Parliament of Western Australia|date=18 May 2022|access-date=27 May 2022}}</ref> [[George Christensen]], previously a Nationals member, did not re-contest the seat of [[Division of Dawson|Dawson]] but ran instead for the Senate for One Nation.

The following Members of Parliament (MPs) and Senators did not contest the election.
